    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado's data center footprint, across 17 facilities, reports only 18 MW of total capacity, and a single facility, Flexential Denver - Englewood, accounts for all 18 MW in the dataset.\n\nThat single-facility attribution almost certainly reflects incomplete reporting rather than physical reality. Eleven of the 17 facilities carry no power figure at all, including a 759,000-square-foot Flexential building in downtown Denver whose capacity is unrecorded here. The true aggregate for the state is unknown from this data alone.\n\nWhat the data does confirm is the geographic shape of Colorado's deployment. Facilities cluster in a corridor running from Colorado Springs through the Denver metro suburbs of Centennial, Englewood, and Aurora, with isolated facilities in Thornton, Broomfield, and two El Paso County locations. Operators range from colocation providers to enterprises running captive infrastructure, including Google, Walmart, JPMorgan Chase, and SAP. That mix of carrier-neutral and single-tenant facilities matters for AI workloads because captive facilities are typically sized and powered for a specific operator's demand, while colocation campuses absorb demand from many tenants and are more sensitive to regional grid constraints.\n\nThe data does not address power availability, grid interconnection timelines, or whether any facility is expanding. It names operators and locations, and not much else.",
